  <title>Browse mode and focus mode</title>
  <p>
    When reading document content, Orca uses two modes: browse mode and focus mode.
  </p>
  <p>
    Browse mode lets Orca provide navigation for reading documents and web content. Focus mode
    lets the application handle its navigation keys instead. Other Orca commands, including
    table navigation and commands using the <key>Orca Modifier</key>, remain available.
  </p>
  <p>
    Orca can switch to focus mode automatically when navigation lands on an interactive widget.
    You can enable or disable automatic focus mode separately for native navigation, caret
    navigation, and structural navigation in Documents preferences.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sticky modes override automatic switching. Sticky focus mode keeps Orca in focus mode until
    you explicitly change modes. Sticky browse mode keeps Orca in browse mode until you
    explicitly change modes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some web applications, including desktop applications built with the Electron web framework,
    start in sticky focus mode so their own keyboard interactions work. Configure this with
    Automatic sticky focus mode for web applications in the Native navigation section of
    Documents preferences.
  </p>
